          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe about starting a statin, and which risk bands are used?",
          "answer": "Guidance on lipids says the statin decision is driven mainly by **estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk**, not by the LDL cholesterol number alone. This comes from the document \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\" (major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary).\n\nThe same document describes screening. It uses a lipid panel, and a non-fasting sample is acceptable for most adults. The usual screening interval is every four to six years for adults aged 20 to 75 who are low risk and not on lipid-lowering medicine.\n\nThe passages I retrieved stop before they name the risk bands or set out when a statin is started, so I can't tell you those categories from this evidence. A different hypertension guideline mentions a 10-year risk threshold of 10 percent, but it applies to decisions about blood-pressure medicine, not statins, so I have not used it here.\n\nTo learn the risk bands and starting thresholds, ask a clinician or pharmacist, or read the full lipid guideline. A clinician can apply those bands to your own results.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
          "risk_level": "routine",
